  "body": "## What does this change?\n\nAdds `deletion_protection_enabled` and `point_in_time_recovery_enabled` to both `single-version-store` and `multi-version-store`, wired through to the `aws_dynamodb_table` resource. Both default to `false`, which is what the module did before, and the three callers pin the module by tag, so nothing changes for them until a root bumps its `ref`.\n\nA VHS table can end up holding the only copy of a source dataset once its adapter stops running. That is happening now to `vhs-calm-adapter`, which is being switched off under wellcomecollection/platform#6689 while its 408,710 records stay in production use, and `vhs-sierra-sierra-adapter-20200604` and `vhs-sourcedata-miro` reach the same point as their sources are retired. Neither of the DynamoDB controls that guards against losing a table is reachable through this module at any tag, including current main.\n\nSetting deletion protection by hand does not hold either, because it is an optional, non-computed attribute on `aws_dynamodb_table` (checked against the hashicorp/aws 6.28.0 provider schema), so an apply of that root that does not declare it turns it back off.\n\n`RELEASE.md` carries `RELEASE_TYPE: minor`, so merging cuts v4.3.0.\n\n## How to test\n\n`terraform fmt -recursive` is clean. Otherwise, plan a root that consumes the module at this ref: with neither variable set the plan is empty, and setting either to `true` gives an in-place update on the table.\n\n## How can we measure success?\n\nThe snapshot work in wellcomecollection/platform#6689 can turn both controls on in terraform rather than in the console, and `describe-table` and `describe-continuous-backups` show them enabled after that apply.\n\n## Have we considered potential risks?\n\n`point_in_time_recovery` is optional and computed in the provider, so the module currently leaves whatever is set out of band alone. Declaring the block changes that, and a caller who moves to v4.3.0 with PITR on out of band would have it turned off unless they also set `point_in_time_recovery_enabled = true`. All three VHS tables have PITR disabled today, so there is nothing to lose in practice.\n\nDeletion protection blocks `terraform destroy` of the table once it is on, which is the point of it, but a root that enables it has to disable it again before any intentional teardown.\n",
